1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,820
The rate of reaction is how fast or slowly a reaction happens.

2
00:00:03,820 --> 00:00:09,035
Fireworks and airbags react in a split second; an airbag inflates in 30 milliseconds!

3
00:00:09,035 --> 00:00:13,886
Rusting and food decaying are slow. Some medicines must act fast, others slowly.

4
00:00:14,947 --> 00:00:19,616
To measure the rate, collect the gas made in a gas syringe at regular times,

5
00:00:19,616 --> 00:00:22,102
or measure the mass lost as gas escapes.

6
00:00:22,102 --> 00:00:27,499
On the graph, the steepest part is the fastest; when it's flat, the reaction has stopped.

7
00:00:28,560 --> 00:00:34,866
Marble chips react with dilute hydrochloric acid, and the carbon dioxide is collected in a gas syringe.

8
00:00:34,866 --> 00:00:37,049
The volume is read every 30 seconds.

9
00:00:38,110 --> 00:00:44,477
In the first 30 seconds, 24 cubic centimetres of gas are collected. In the next 30 seconds, only 16 more.

10
00:00:45,537 --> 00:00:51,177
The curve gets less and less steep, and after 150 seconds it is flat at 58 cubic centimetres.

11
00:00:52,237 --> 00:00:58,423
Pause and decide: what was the average rate in the first 30 seconds, and why does the curve level off?

12
00:00:59,483 --> 00:01:01,969
Reactions happen when particles collide.

13
00:01:01,969 --> 00:01:07,427
But only effective collisions make products: the particles must meet the right way round,

14
00:01:07,427 --> 00:01:13,733
like puzzle pieces, and with enough energy to break bonds. This minimum energy is the activation energy.

15
00:01:14,794 --> 00:01:20,918
Mix vinegar and baking soda and it fizzes fast at first, because there are many particles colliding.

16
00:01:20,918 --> 00:01:27,346
As they're used up, there are fewer collisions, so the reaction slows, and stops when a reactant runs out.
